{'id': 'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-external-auditory-canal-cholesteatoma', 'domain': 'Otology / Neurotology', 'topic': 'External Auditory Canal Cholesteatoma', 'choices': [], 'answer': None, 'explanation': 'This check tests the manage layer of External Auditory Canal Cholesteatoma. The reveal comes from the live canonical Deep Curriculum, so the question is testing the concept itself rather than a generic red-flag template.', 'board_pearl': 'EACC is focal and erosive: keratin plus localized bony erosion/sequestration in the canal. That separates it from keratosis obturans, which classically produces a dense circumferential keratin plug with generalized smooth canal widening rather than focal osteitic destruction. A destructive canal lesion also demands a malignancy/NOE differential when the host, pain, or soft tissue is atypical.', 'curveball': 'The defining process is in the external auditory canal rather than the tympanic membrane retraction pocket or middle ear. Disease often begins along one wall of the bony canal and erodes focally; inferior and posterior canal walls are common locations.', 'tier': 'Concept check', 'mode': 'Recognize', 'concept_id': 'v6-otology-neurotology-external-auditory-canal-cholesteatoma', 'assessment_class': 'recall', 'graded_discrimination': False, 'prompt': "A patient's presentation is consistent with External Auditory Canal Cholesteatoma. External auditory canal cholesteatoma (EACC) is focal accumulation of keratinizing squamous epithelium in the bony EAC with underlying osteitis, focal bony erosion, and sometimes sequestration. Patients commonly have chronic otorrhea, dull otalgia, conductive hearing loss, or an incidentally seen focal keratin pocket. The tympanic membrane and middle ear may initially be normal. EACC may be spontaneous or secondary to canal trauma, surgery, radiation, stenosis, or chronic obstruction. What is the initial recognition and management strategy, and what finding, response, or failure would change the next step?", 'answer_text': 'Small accessible EACC without deep extension can sometimes be managed with meticulous microscopic debridement, dry-ear care, treatment of secondary infection, and serial surveillance. Persistent focal disease, progressive erosion, pain/drainage despite office care, canal stenosis, or disease that cannot be safely cleaned favors operative eradication. Unlike keratosis obturans, the management problem is not merely removing a circumferential plug; it is controlling a focal erosive epithelial process and restoring a self-cleaning canal.', 'recall_source': 'Deep Curriculum', 'board_style_v177': True, 'board_dimension_v177': 'manage', 'curveball_answer': 'Surgery is tailored to extent. Limited disease may require transcanal removal with canalplasty and coverage of exposed bone using local skin, fascia, or grafting as appropriate. More extensive posterior disease may require a postauricular approach and mastoidectomy if mastoid air cells or middle ear are involved. Preserve facial nerve, temporomandibular joint capsule, tympanic membrane, ossicles, and healthy canal skin whenever possible;', 'converted_to_oral_board_v178': True, 'gapfill_v438': True},
