{'id': 'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-facial-nerve-schwannoma', 'domain': 'Otology / Neurotology', 'topic': 'Facial Nerve Schwannoma', 'choices': [], 'answer': None, 'explanation': 'This check tests the manage layer of Facial Nerve Schwannoma. The reveal comes from the live canonical Deep Curriculum, so the question is testing the concept itself rather than a generic red-flag template.', 'board_pearl': "Recurrent same-side palsy or failure of presumed Bell palsy to recover warrants imaging. Counsel from the patient's present function, not tumor size alone, and explain the facial-function tradeoff of intervention.", 'curveball': 'The tumor can arise along any facial-nerve segment; smooth fallopian-canal enlargement and multisegment enhancement help distinguish it from other temporal-bone and cerebellopontine-angle lesions.', 'tier': 'Concept check', 'mode': 'Recognize', 'concept_id': 'v6-otology-neurotology-facial-nerve-schwannoma', 'assessment_class': 'recall', 'graded_discrimination': False, 'prompt': "A patient's presentation is consistent with Facial Nerve Schwannoma. Think of facial nerve schwannoma with slowly progressive, recurrent, or fluctuating ipsilateral facial weakness, a middle-ear or internal-auditory-canal mass, or facial function discordant with tumor size. What is the initial recognition and management strategy, and what finding, response, or failure would change the next step?", 'answer_text': 'Observe small or slowly growing tumors with useful facial function using serial examination, audiometry, and MRI. Stereotactic radiation or surgery is individualized by growth, symptoms, hearing, location, age, and goals. Contrast MRI maps neural and soft-tissue extent; temporal-bone CT defines fallopian-canal and ossicular involvement. Document serial facial grade, hearing, growth, and electrodiagnostic findings when useful.', 'recall_source': 'Deep Curriculum', 'board_style_v177': True, 'board_dimension_v177': 'manage', 'curveball_answer': 'Options include decompression, nerve-sparing debulking in selected anatomy, radiosurgery, or resection with primary repair, grafting, or nerve transfer. Resection often risks major facial deterioration, so intact function argues against reflexive excision.', 'converted_to_oral_board_v178': True, 'gapfill_v438': True},
