{'id': 'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-auditory-neuropathy-spectrum-disorder', 'domain': 'Otology / Neurotology', 'topic': 'Auditory Neuropathy Spectrum Disorder', 'choices': [], 'answer': None, 'explanation': 'This check tests the manage layer of Auditory Neuropathy Spectrum Disorder. The reveal comes from the live canonical Deep Curriculum, so the question is testing the concept itself rather than a generic red-flag template.', 'board_pearl': 'The signature is preserved cochlear receptor activity with disordered neural synchrony. Base escalation on functional speech-language progress, not a single audiogram.', 'curveball': 'Outer-hair-cell function is preserved while inner-hair-cell, synaptic, or auditory-nerve transmission is dys-synchronous; lesion site strongly affects rehabilitation prognosis.', 'tier': 'Concept check', 'mode': 'Recognize', 'concept_id': 'v6-otology-neurotology-auditory-neuropathy-spectrum-disorder', 'assessment_class': 'recall', 'graded_discrimination': False, 'prompt': "A patient's presentation is consistent with Auditory Neuropathy Spectrum Disorder. Suspect ANSD when speech understanding is disproportionately poor or fluctuating relative to behavioral thresholds, especially with prematurity, hyperbilirubinemia, neuropathy, or family history. What is the initial recognition and management strategy, and what finding, response, or failure would change the next step?", 'answer_text': 'Provide early language access and individualized hearing-aid or remote-microphone trials with close functional monitoring; thresholds alone do not predict benefit. Confirm a present cochlear microphonic and/or otoacoustic emissions with absent or markedly abnormal ABR, recognizing that OAEs may disappear over time. Add behavioral audiology, speech perception, genetics, and MRI of the cochlear nerves.', 'recall_source': 'Deep Curriculum', 'board_style_v177': True, 'board_dimension_v177': 'manage', 'curveball_answer': 'Consider cochlear implantation when appropriately fitted amplification fails to support speech and language. Confirm cochlear-nerve integrity because severe deficiency predicts limited CI benefit and may prompt auditory-brainstem-implant evaluation in selected centers.', 'converted_to_oral_board_v178': True, 'gapfill_v438': True},
{'id': 'cc-v112-mgt-otology-neurotology-auditory-neuropathy-spectrum-disorder', 'domain': 'Otology / Neurotology', 'topic': 'Auditory Neuropathy Spectrum Disorder', 'stem': 'A patient has been diagnosed with Auditory Neuropathy Spectrum Disorder. Based on the current curriculum framework, which management principle is most appropriate?', 'choices': ['Eye protection is immediate. Treat idiopathic acute palsy promptly with corticosteroids; manage Ramsay Hunt, infection, trauma or neoplasm according to cause.', 'Provide early language access and individualized hearing-aid or remote-microphone trials with close functional monitoring; thresholds alone do not predict benefit.', 'Observe stable incidental lesions or drain/resect according to diagnosis, symptoms, growth and access corridor.', 'Treat the systemic driver with the relevant team plus symptom-specific hearing/vestibular rehabilitation; urgent sudden or rapidly progressive deficits keep their time-sensitive pathways.'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Provide early language access and individualized hearing-aid or remote-microphone trials with close functional monitoring; thresholds alone do not predict benefit.', 'why_wrong': ['This strategy belongs more closely to Facial Paralysis; it does not address the defining management problem in Auditory Neuropathy Spectrum Disorder.', 'Correct.', 'This strategy belongs more closely to Petrous Apex Lesions; it does not address the defining management problem in Auditory Neuropathy Spectrum Disorder.', 'This strategy belongs more closely to Otologic Manifestations of Systemic Disease; it does not address the defining management problem in Auditory Neuropathy Spectrum Disorder.'], 'board_pearl': 'The signature is preserved cochlear receptor activity with disordered neural synchrony. Base escalation on functional speech-language progress, not a single audiogram.', 'curveball': 'Outer-hair-cell function is preserved while inner-hair-cell, synaptic, or auditory-nerve transmission is dys-synchronous; lesion site strongly affects rehabilitation prognosis.', 'tier': 'Concept check', 'mode': 'Manage', 'concept_id': 'v6-otology-neurotology-auditory-neuropathy-spectrum-disorder', 'assessment_class': 'recall', 'graded_discrimination': False, 'curveball_answer': 'Consider cochlear implantation when appropriately fitted amplification fails to support speech and language. Confirm cochlear-nerve integrity because severe deficiency predicts limited CI benefit and may prompt auditory-brainstem-implant evaluation in selected centers.', 'gapfill_v438': True},
{'id': 'cc-v112-rec-otology-neurotology-vernet-syndrome-jugular-foramen-syndrome', 'domain': 'Otology / Neurotology', 'topic': 'Vernet Syndrome (Jugular Foramen Syndrome)', 'choices': [], 'answer': None, 'explanation': 'This check tests the manage layer of Vernet Syndrome (Jugular Foramen Syndrome). The reveal comes from the live canonical Deep Curriculum, so the question is testing the concept itself rather than a generic red-flag template.', 'board_pearl': "Boards pearl: build the lower-cranial-nerve 'syndrome ladder.' IX + X + XI at the jugular foramen = Vernet. Add XII (hypoglossal canal) = Collet-Sicard. Add Horner syndrome from sympathetic-chain involvement in the retroparotid space = Villaret. This ladder is a classic oral-board discrimination question.", 'curveball': 'The lesion sits at the jugular foramen itself, where IX, X, and XI exit the skull base together; CN XII exits separately through the hypoglossal canal. Adding hypoglossal (XII) involvement to this picture is Collet-Sicard syndrome;', 'tier': 'Concept check', 'mode': 'Recognize', 'concept_id': 'v6-otology-neurotology-vernet-syndrome-jugular-foramen-syndrome', 'assessment_class': 'recall', 'graded_discrimination': False, 'prompt': "A patient's presentation is consistent with Vernet Syndrome (Jugular Foramen Syndrome). Ipsilateral CN IX, X, and XI palsy: loss of gag reflex and posterior-tongue taste (IX), hoarseness, vocal-fold paralysis, and dysphagia (X), and shoulder droop with weak head-turn to the opposite side from trapezius/sternocleidomastoid weakness (XI). CN XII (tongue) is spared -- its involvement instead defines Collet-Sicard syndrome. What is the initial recognition and management strategy, and what finding, response, or failure would change the next step?", 'answer_text': 'Decide between observation (favored for small, asymptomatic, slow-growing lesions, especially in older or comorbid patients, since resection risks worsening existing lower-cranial-neuropathy), stereotactic radiosurgery, or surgical resection based on lesion type, growth, and symptom burden. Contrast MRI/MRA/CTA of the skull base characterizes the jugular foramen mass and its vascularity; paraganglioma workup includes catheter angiography and embolization planning when appropriate. Add direct laryngoscopy to confirm vocal-fold mobility and a formal swallow evaluation.', 'recall_source': 'Deep Curriculum', 'board_style_v177': True, 'board_dimension_v177': 'manage', 'curveball_answer': 'Vascular imaging is mandatory before biopsying a suspected paraganglioma given its hypervascularity. Approach and exposure depend on the specific lesion (e.g., infratemporal fossa approaches for jugular foramen tumors);', 'converted_to_oral_board_v178': True, 'gapfill_v438': True},
