{'id': 'cc-v112-rec-thyroid-parathyroid-salivary-familial-hyperparathyroidism-and-parathyromatosis', 'domain': 'Thyroid / Parathyroid / Salivary', 'topic': 'Familial Hyperparathyroidism and Parathyromatosis', 'choices': [], 'answer': None, 'explanation': 'This check tests the manage layer of Familial Hyperparathyroidism and Parathyromatosis. The reveal comes from the live canonical Deep Curriculum, so the question is testing the concept itself rather than a generic red-flag template.', 'board_pearl': "Young age, multigland disease and recurrence are clues that 'one bad adenoma' may be the wrong mental model; the biology determines the operation and the lifetime surveillance plan.", 'curveball': 'Hereditary syndromes such as MEN1, MEN2A, CDC73-related disease and familial isolated hyperparathyroidism alter the expected gland distribution and recurrence risk. Parathyromatosis refers to multiple implants/foci of hyperfunctioning parathyroid tissue, sometimes after capsular disruption or seeding.', 'tier': 'Concept check', 'mode': 'Recognize', 'concept_id': 'v6-thyroid-parathyroid-salivary-familial-hyperparathyroidism-and-parathyromatosis', 'assessment_class': 'recall', 'graded_discrimination': False, 'prompt': "A patient's presentation is consistent with Familial Hyperparathyroidism and Parathyromatosis. Primary hyperparathyroidism at a young age, multigland disease, recurrent disease, family history, jaw tumors, pituitary/pancreatic tumors, or unusual parathyroid pathology should prompt consideration of a hereditary syndrome; recurrent hyperparathyroidism after prior parathyroid surgery can also rarely reflect parathyromatosis. What is the initial recognition and management strategy, and what finding, response, or failure would change the next step?", 'answer_text': 'Management is syndrome- and anatomy-specific. Counsel that multigland/hereditary disease has different recurrence expectations from a solitary sporadic adenoma, and that reoperative disease should be managed in an experienced center. Review calcium/PTH chronology, prior operative and pathology reports, family history and syndromic features; pursue syndrome-directed genetic evaluation when indicated and use localization imaging to guide reoperative planning rather than to establish the biochemical diagnosis.', 'recall_source': 'Deep Curriculum', 'board_style_v177': True, 'board_dimension_v177': 'manage', 'curveball_answer': 'Avoid rupturing a parathyroid adenoma or spilling tissue. In reoperative disease, identify the expected recurrent laryngeal nerve course, use prior operative information and localization, and tailor the operation to hereditary versus sporadic biology.', 'converted_to_oral_board_v178': True, 'gapfill_v438': True},
{'id': 'cc-v112-mgt-thyroid-parathyroid-salivary-familial-hyperparathyroidism-and-parathyromatosis', 'domain': 'Thyroid / Parathyroid / Salivary', 'topic': 'Familial Hyperparathyroidism and Parathyromatosis', 'stem': 'A patient has been diagnosed with Familial Hyperparathyroidism and Parathyromatosis. Based on the current curriculum framework, which management principle is most appropriate?', 'choices': ['Management is NEPHROLOGY-FIRST and physiology-directed. Correct modifiable phosphate/calcium/vitamin-D abnormalities and optimize dialysis/CKD-MBD therapy.', 'Management is syndrome- and anatomy-specific. Counsel that multigland/hereditary disease has different recurrence expectations from a solitary sporadic adenoma, and that reoperative disease should be managed in an experienced center.', 'Coordinate prophylactic/therapeutic thyroid surgery and family screening with endocrinology/genetics.', 'Coordinate thyroid control, smoking cessation and activity/severity-directed medical/ophthalmic therapy.'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Management is syndrome- and anatomy-specific. Counsel that multigland/hereditary disease has different recurrence expectations from a solitary sporadic adenoma, and that reoperative disease should be managed in an experienced center.', 'why_wrong': ['This strategy belongs more closely to Secondary / Tertiary Hyperparathyroidism; it does not address the defining management problem in Familial Hyperparathyroidism and Parathyromatosis.', 'Correct.', 'This strategy belongs more closely to MEN2 / RET; it does not address the defining management problem in Familial Hyperparathyroidism and Parathyromatosis.', 'This strategy belongs more closely to Thyroid Eye Disease / Graves Ophthalmopathy; it does not address the defining management problem in Familial Hyperparathyroidism and Parathyromatosis.'], 'board_pearl': "Young age, multigland disease and recurrence are clues that 'one bad adenoma' may be the wrong mental model; the biology determines the operation and the lifetime surveillance plan.", 'curveball': 'Hereditary syndromes such as MEN1, MEN2A, CDC73-related disease and familial isolated hyperparathyroidism alter the expected gland distribution and recurrence risk. Parathyromatosis refers to multiple implants/foci of hyperfunctioning parathyroid tissue, sometimes after capsular disruption or seeding.', 'tier': 'Concept check', 'mode': 'Manage', 'concept_id': 'v6-thyroid-parathyroid-salivary-familial-hyperparathyroidism-and-parathyromatosis', 'assessment_class': 'recall', 'graded_discrimination': False, 'curveball_answer': 'Avoid rupturing a parathyroid adenoma or spilling tissue. In reoperative disease, identify the expected recurrent laryngeal nerve course, use prior operative information and localization, and tailor the operation to hereditary versus sporadic biology.', 'gapfill_v438': True},
{'id': 'cc-v112-rec-thyroid-parathyroid-salivary-salivary-adenoid-cystic-carcinoma-and-perineural-spread', 'domain': 'Thyroid / Parathyroid / Salivary', 'topic': 'Salivary Adenoid Cystic Carcinoma and Perineural Spread', 'choices': [], 'answer': None, 'explanation': 'This check tests the manage layer of Salivary Adenoid Cystic Carcinoma and Perineural Spread. The reveal comes from the live canonical Deep Curriculum, so the question is testing the concept itself rather than a generic red-flag template.', 'board_pearl': 'Pain or neuropathy in a salivary tumor is an oncologic localizing sign. Think along the nerve all the way to the skull base, not just around the palpable gland mass.', 'curveball': 'Adenoid cystic carcinoma has a strong propensity for perineural spread along named cranial nerves toward the skull base and for late distant metastasis, particularly to lung.', 'tier': 'Concept check', 'mode': 'Recognize', 'concept_id': 'v6-thyroid-parathyroid-salivary-salivary-adenoid-cystic-carcinoma-and-perineural-spread', 'assessment_class': 'recall', 'graded_discrimination': False, 'prompt': "A patient's presentation is consistent with Salivary Adenoid Cystic Carcinoma and Perineural Spread. A salivary gland malignancy with disproportionate pain, numbness, weakness, or a long infiltrative course should raise concern for a histology notorious for perineural invasion and spread along cranial nerves, sometimes recurring locally years after apparently complete resection. What is the initial recognition and management strategy, and what finding, response, or failure would change the next step?", 'answer_text': 'Primary treatment is surgical resection when feasible, commonly followed by postoperative radiation for adverse features including perineural invasion. Long-term surveillance matters because recurrence and distant metastasis can occur late. Map cranial nerve deficits on exam and obtain contrast MRI with attention to the involved nerve pathway and skull base when perineural spread is suspected; stage the primary, neck and chest according to disease extent.', 'recall_source': 'Deep Curriculum', 'board_style_v177': True, 'board_dimension_v177': 'manage', 'curveball_answer': 'Resection must follow the disease rather than stopping at an arbitrary gland boundary; grossly involved nerve may require sacrifice toward a negative proximal margin when oncologically appropriate, with reconstruction/rehabilitation planned in advance.', 'converted_to_oral_board_v178': True, 'gapfill_v438': True},
{'id': 'cc-v112-mgt-thyroid-parathyroid-salivary-salivary-adenoid-cystic-carcinoma-and-perineural-spread', 'domain': 'Thyroid / Parathyroid / Salivary', 'topic': 'Salivary Adenoid Cystic Carcinoma and Perineural Spread', 'stem': 'A patient has been diagnosed with Salivary Adenoid Cystic Carcinoma and Perineural Spread. Based on the current curriculum framework, which management principle is most appropriate?', 'choices': ['Do not treat radiographic existence alone. Stable/asymptomatic RAIR disease can often be monitored; oligoprogressive or symptomatic focal disease may be controlled with surgery, directed radiation or image-guided ablation to defer systemic toxicity.', 'Primary treatment is surgical resection when feasible, commonly followed by postoperative radiation for adverse features including perineural invasion.', 'Current management is deliberately de-escalated for appropriate low-risk disease. Lobectomy is sufficient/preferred for many unilateral low-risk cancers and routine RAI is not indicated for low-risk DTC simply because total thyroidectomy was performed.', 'Management is NEPHROLOGY-FIRST and physiology-directed. Correct modifiable phosphate/calcium/vitamin-D abnormalities and optimize dialysis/CKD-MBD therapy.'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Primary treatment is surgical resection when feasible, commonly followed by postoperative radiation for adverse features including perineural invasion. Long-term surveillance matters because recurrence and distant metastasis can occur late.', 'why_wrong': ['This strategy belongs more closely to Radioiodine-Refractory Differentiated Thyroid Cancer; it does not address the defining management problem in Salivary Adenoid Cystic Carcinoma and Perineural Spread.', 'Correct.', 'This strategy belongs more closely to Differentiated Thyroid Cancer; it does not address the defining management problem in Salivary Adenoid Cystic Carcinoma and Perineural Spread.', 'This strategy belongs more closely to Secondary / Tertiary Hyperparathyroidism; it does not address the defining management problem in Salivary Adenoid Cystic Carcinoma and Perineural Spread.'], 'board_pearl': 'Pain or neuropathy in a salivary tumor is an oncologic localizing sign. Think along the nerve all the way to the skull base, not just around the palpable gland mass.', 'curveball': 'Adenoid cystic carcinoma has a strong propensity for perineural spread along named cranial nerves toward the skull base and for late distant metastasis, particularly to lung.', 'tier': 'Concept check', 'mode': 'Manage', 'concept_id': 'v6-thyroid-parathyroid-salivary-salivary-adenoid-cystic-carcinoma-and-perineural-spread', 'assessment_class': 'recall', 'graded_discrimination': False, 'curveball_answer': 'Resection must follow the disease rather than stopping at an arbitrary gland boundary; grossly involved nerve may require sacrifice toward a negative proximal margin when oncologically appropriate, with reconstruction/rehabilitation planned in advance.', 'gapfill_v438': True},
{'id': 'cc-v112-rec-thyroid-parathyroid-salivary-subacute-de-quervain-thyroiditis', 'domain': 'Thyroid / Parathyroid / Salivary', 'topic': 'Subacute (de Quervain) Thyroiditis', 'choices': [], 'answer': None, 'explanation': 'This check tests the manage layer of Subacute (de Quervain) Thyroiditis. The reveal comes from the live canonical Deep Curriculum, so the question is testing the concept itself rather than a generic red-flag template.', 'board_pearl': 'Pain, high inflammatory markers, thyrotoxicosis, and low uptake form the classic pattern; anticipate the thyrotoxic-to-hypothyroid-to-recovery sequence.', 'curveball': 'Inflammatory follicular disruption releases stored hormone; this is destructive thyroiditis, not increased hormone synthesis.', 'tier': 'Concept check', 'mode': 'Recognize', 'concept_id': 'v6-thyroid-parathyroid-salivary-subacute-de-quervain-thyroiditis', 'assessment_class': 'recall', 'graded_discrimination': False, 'prompt': "A patient's presentation is consistent with Subacute (de Quervain) Thyroiditis. A painful tender thyroid after a viral-like illness, fever, and transient thyrotoxic symptoms suggests subacute granulomatous thyroiditis. What is the initial recognition and management strategy, and what finding, response, or failure would change the next step?", 'answer_text': 'Treat pain with NSAIDs and use corticosteroids for severe or refractory inflammation. Beta blockade treats adrenergic symptoms; antithyroid drugs do not help. Follow for the transient hypothyroid phase and uncommon permanent hypothyroidism. Check TSH/free hormones and ESR or CRP. Low radioactive-iodine uptake during thyrotoxicosis distinguishes destructive thyroiditis from Graves disease; ultrasound or biopsy is reserved for atypical or focal findings.', 'recall_source': 'Deep Curriculum', 'board_style_v177': True, 'board_dimension_v177': 'manage', 'curveball_answer': 'There is no routine surgical role. Failure to follow the expected course, a persistent focal lesion, abscess concern, or compressive progression should reopen the diagnosis.', 'converted_to_oral_board_v178': True, 'gapfill_v438': True},
